<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John, Duke of Berry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1340">1340</a>–<a href="/years/1416">1416</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John of Berry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John, Duke of Berry, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> who held Berry, Auvergne and Poitou as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>. He was a leading figure in the regency for his nephew Charles VI and one of the greatest art patrons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, commissioning the illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> known as the Très Riches Heures. On his death in 1416 his appanages, including Poitou, returned to the crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry.jpg" alt="John, Duke of Berry" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petrusbarbygere · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duc_de_Berry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
